Read: `dao.lease.md` (under review), `datom.world.md`, `jit-design.md`, `dao.stream.md`, `dao.stream.ws.md`. No edits made.

# Findings

**Nothing at Critical severity.** The document is coherent in the large; every finding below is fixable with sentences, not restructuring.

**MAJOR | "Judging lapse" (ll. 235–244) + "A lease is facts" (l. 129) | the lapse predicate is undefined between grant and first renewal.** The judge's state is "the newest renewal it has observed per lease"; the duration is "the silence tolerated after each observed renewal." A holder that dies before its first renewal — which the holder's own discipline ("renews at a fraction of the duration") makes a normal case, and which is exactly the canonical dead-holder scenario the Purpose section exists for — produces a lease the judging rules cannot evaluate: no renewal observed, therefore no interval, therefore no lapse condition. Either never-renewed leases can never lapse (a leak, defeating the document) or every implementer will silently seed the window from the grant. **Correction:** one sentence in *Judging lapse*: observing the `:accepted` fact seeds the same state an observed renewal seeds — the measured interval runs from the judge's observation of the grant or of the newest renewal, whichever is later.

**MAJOR | "Judging lapse" (ll. 236–238) | "derived from the stream, rebuildable from it" is false, and contradicted twice inside this document.** Renewals "carry no time" (l. 159), so the judge's observation times are judge-local facts that are provably not in the stream — the restart bullets (ll. 248–254) exist precisely because the state is *not* rebuildable. Worse, the derivable half fails too: under oldest-first eviction the `:accepted` fact is evicted before any renewal of that lease, taking the duration and cap with it, so a judge restarting after grant eviction sees renewals naming a lease whose terms it cannot recover — it can neither judge nor even identify its obligation, and the sizing relation "the medium's retention window exceeds the judge's lag" bounds runtime lag, not restart-after-eviction. A judge whose restarts outpace one duration (crash-loop, frequent redeploy) resets to *unknown* forever and leaks while every component reports healthy — the obligation's "within duration plus cadence" (l. 280) is silently conditional on the judge completing a full duration between faults. **Correction:** strike "rebuildable from it." Split the judge's duties: terms must be durable (persisted, or restated by the grantor in a form the exactly-once rule permits), observation times are never rebuildable and volatile loss mandates the one-full-duration *unknown* rule; state the crash-loop condition on the obligation's promise explicitly.

**MAJOR | "Out of scope — Carriage" (ll. 384–390) vs `dao.stream.ws.md` "Ending a served stream" (ll. 199–209) and Deferred (ll. 373–379) | "a reclaimed connection closes with a distinguishing close code" asserts a mechanism ws.md does not grant, and collides with the one distinction its close code exists to make.** In ws.md the distinguishing close code means exactly one thing: the served stream ended — the client deposits `:ws/ended` and must not reattach. Where reclaim coincides with ending a per-connection served stream, the sentence is redundant but harmless. Where the served stream is shared (ws.md's own multiplexing case: "two connections to the same served stream answer identically"), reclaiming one attachment's lease cannot end the stream, ws.md gives the server no per-attachment close code, and the client — per ws.md's own discipline, no frame ⇒ reattach — will reattach, undoing the lease's judgment at the transport layer. The lease document is prescribing content for ws.md's deferred close-code space while claiming carriage "belongs to the transport documents." **Correction:** either drop the sentence and let reclamation reach the holder as data (the `:lapsed` fact riding as ordinary payload — the option consistent with "the transport never learns lease vocabulary"), or amend the carriage paragraph to require ws.md to distinguish *reclaim* from *ended* from *dropped*, and say in the scope claim that this is the one place the document reaches into ws.md's deferred design. Note also ws.md's Deferred explicitly awaits a "durable home" for a pause vocabulary — a seat this document plausibly occupies and never claims.

**MAJOR | "Renewal is evidence" (ll. 169–173) | "streams are single-writer and boundary events are attributed, so authorship is the ambient capability this architecture already has."** dao.stream.md's Concurrency section (ll. 588–599) does not grant single-writer streams — it contemplates multiple writers and defers coordination ("a one-log-per-writer discipline") to interpretation, i.e., to composition. The medium records no provenance; ws.md has attribution only because its envelope carries `:ws/attachment` — composed data, not an ambient property. On an unattributed shared medium (the third named use, "a claim an agent leaves on shared work," is precisely this), "Only grantor-authored facts establish terms" is unenforceable: any writer can append `:accepted` for its own id and grant itself tenure. **Correction:** replace "ambient" with a composition duty: the composition owes fact attribution — one medium per author, an author key in the envelope, or transport attribution as ws provides — and a judged medium without it is a host assembly defect of the same rank as the unwired judge (see also the Minor finding on the precedent claim: this is the unnamed fourth divergence).

**MODERATE | "Judging lapse" gap bullet (ll. 246–248) | "Eviction is oldest-first, so if any renewal for a lease is retained, the newest is."** dao.stream.md standardizes no eviction policy — oldest-first is a ring-buffer example (l. 605) and a ws deposit-destination admission rule, not a contract guarantee. Under a medium that evicts arbitrarily, the gap-recovery rule is unsafe, not merely conservative: a renewal appended and evicted before the judge read it, while an older one is retained, leaves the judge's "newest observed" stale, inflates the measured interval, and false-lapses a live holder — the exact failure the observed-window discipline exists to prevent. **Correction:** scope it — require judged media to declare evict-oldest (a host assembly rule, like ws.md's deposit admission), or make the rule medium-independent: after any gap, treat the lease as *unknown* for one full duration regardless of what re-reading recovers.

**MODERATE | "Whose clock decides" (ll. 215–222) | "skew cannot enter it. Skew becomes transit delay, and delay errs safe... the grantor tolerates at least as long as the holder expects."** Offset skew is indeed eliminated — the comparison is on one clock. Rate skew is not: the holder generates its cadence on its clock, the judge measures tolerance on its own, and a judge clock running fast relative to the holder's shortens real-time tolerance below the holder's expectation — a false lapse with zero transit delay. The remedy (renew at a fraction; "duration exceeds twice the holder's renewal interval") is a skew budget, but it is presented only as a load/flight-time relation. Relatedly, the document names "a slow pipe under the last renewal" as the dangerous direction but never draws its conclusion: a partition or delivery stall outlasting the duration destroys a live grant whose holder performed its full duty — under ws.md, `append!` `ok` on an outbound path implies nothing about delivery. **Correction:** replace "skew cannot enter it" with "offset skew cannot enter the comparison; rate skew remains and is absorbed by the renewal fraction, so the sizing relation is a skew budget as well as a load budget," and add one sentence naming partition-outlasting-duration as the design's hard limit, the holder's remedy being re-negotiation on reconnect.

**MINOR | "Whose obligation" (ll. 264–267) | "a grantor that never runs again... its resources end with its process."** True for the named process-backed uses (socket, in-memory table); false as stated for durable resources — a reserved slot on disk, a claim on a persistent log — where grantor death leaves the grant held with no judge running: precisely the stuck state the Purpose section set out to end. **Correction:** scope the claim to process-backed resources, or add that a lease over a durable resource requires its judge's durability to match the resource's.

**MINOR | Precedent (ll. 17–59) | "Three things do not carry over, and this document diverges exactly there."** The three named divergences check out against jit-design.md — none is spurious — but "exactly" is false: a fourth divergence is load-bearing and unnamed. The precedent's parties are cooperative and its datoms unattributed; a lease's parties are adversarial and every fact's author is decisive ("Only grantor-authored facts establish terms," "A renewal is evidence about its author," the entire *Renewal forever* and *Delegated renewal* sections). None of the three covers authorship. Secondarily, the mapping quietly splits the precedent's VM across two parties — the holder emits the trace half and proposes (JIT's roles), the grantor applies and judges (VM's roles) — which "The grantor is the VM" states as cleaner than it is. **Correction:** name attribution/adversarity as the fourth divergence (it is where the design does its most original work), and add one honest sentence about the role split.

**MINOR | "A lease is facts" (ll. 111–112) | identity is "unique among that party's leases" — uniqueness scoped to the minting party.** Two holders can mint the same id; the grantor then holds indistinguishable leases. **Correction:** state that uniqueness must hold among all facts the judge will read on the judged medium — ids namespaced by author, or the composition's attribution disambiguating (dependent on the attribution finding above) — and say which.

**MINOR | "Whose obligation" (ll. 269–273) | "refused when the composition is built — exactly as `dao.stream.ws.md` refuses a reject-mode deposit destination."** ws.md has a concrete refusal point: the boundary's construction validates the destination's declared nature. No counterpart exists for "wired no judging interpreter" — no named constructor, validator, or authority could refuse it. The analogy borrows enforcement rhetoric without an enforcement point. **Correction:** either name where the refusal happens, or downgrade to what is true: a defect detectable only by review, with no runtime or assembly-time signature.

**EDITORIAL | three small items.** (1) l. 305: "the 'retention that waits for readers' the migration plan forbids" cites an unglimpsed document with no link; dao.stream.md's own "Eviction never waits on a reader" already suffices — cite that. (2) The vocabulary classifies facts by two dispatch keys, `:dao.lease/status` and `:dao.lease/event`; defensible as the namespace-separation analogue, but a reader must switch on both — say so in the renewal example. (3) `:rejected` is the one status given no justification (`:released` gets one); and renegotiation-implies-a-new-lease-id (forced by "authored exactly once") is never stated.

## Reconciliation verdict (the `:proposed`/`:accepted`/`:rejected` split)

Judged sound, not papered over. Read with "at most one fact per negotiation status" and "authored exactly once," the added rule converts the statuses from a state machine into a record of speech acts: a proposal is something someone said, not something the system holds. That answers the strongest form of the objection — there is no pending state, no queue, no obligation created by the asker's utterance, and silence is absence exactly as everywhere else. The residue is cosmetic, not structural: the vocabulary still *invites* protocol thinking, and `:rejected` does the least work in the table (it buys the holder a definitive negative; under no-pending the holder was owed nothing anyway — one sentence would square this with `:released`'s justification).

## What an implementer must invent

The grant-seeding rule (finding 1); durable judge terms and the restart contract (finding 2); the attribution wiring for the judged medium (finding 4); the driver of judgment points — something must run the judge when no event arrives, and the natural composition (a timer adapter appending tick events to a stream the judge reads, which datom.world's callback rule legitimizes) is nowhere exemplified; the reclaim-vs-ended coordination with the transport (finding 3); identity format and namespacing (Minor); how a solicited grant reaches the holder — only the unsolicited case is called canonical and no pattern is given for the other; the renegotiation-as-new-lease mechanics.

## The thing most likely to be wrong that I cannot prove is wrong

That judgment points are ones the grantor's composition "already passes through." The document's safe-point analogy leans on the VM reaching safe points as a side effect of running; but the judge's runs cannot be arrival-driven — absence of traffic is the very condition it must judge — so something must schedule interpreting when nothing happens. I suspect every real deployment wires a timer-as-event-source solely to drive judging, making the judge a scheduled component in all but name, and the difference between "a clock read at a judgment point" and "a timer that fires" a difference of vocabulary rather than of mechanism. Whether that violates the spirit of "never fires," or is exactly what datom.world's timer-adapter rule blesses, is not decidable from the documents in scope — the runtime scheduling story lives outside them.
